    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d195">

                <pb facs="0504" n="470" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v07p1/pg470"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.62A/6–453: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 195<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of
                            State to the Office of the United States High
                            Commissioner for Germany, at Bonn</hi><anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d195-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret
                        priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-06-04T18:18:00-04:00">June 4, 1953—6:18
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5415. Personal for Reber from Secretary.
                    Please see Adenauer and inform him I
                    discussed Blankenhorn’s visit with
                    President. Re Bermuda meeting, President wishes you to tell Adenauer that purpose is for a general exchange
                    of views rather than to make detailed <pb facs="0505" n="471" xml:id="pg471"/>
                    decisions on variety of problems and that meetings will be informal without
                    agenda.</p>
                <p>While it is obviously impossible to talk about European affairs without
                    references to German problems, President would like to assure Adenauer that no decisions affecting Germany
                    will be taken without full consultation with Adenauer.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d195-f2"/></p>
                <p>President saw Blankenhorn this afternoon
                    and told him he was sending above message to Adenauer.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d195-f3"/></p>
                <p>Cabling summary Blankenhorn’s meeting
                    with President in separate telegram.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d195-f4"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d195-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d195-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted, cleared, and initialed for the Secretary of State by MacArthur.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d195-f2" n="2" xml:id="d195-f2-content">
                <p>On June 5 Reber replied that the
                    President’s message had been delivered to Adenauer who conveyed his sincere thanks for it and who noted
                    with great satisfaction the assurance that no decisions affecting Germany would
                    be taken at Bermuda. (Telegram 5233 from Bonn, 611.62A/6–553)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d195-f3" n="3" xml:id="d195-f3-content">
                <p>For a record of this meeting, see MacArthur’s memorandum of conversation, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d195-f4" n="4" xml:id="d195-f4-content">
                <p>Telegram 5434 to Bonn, June 4. (611.62A/6–453)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
